C["articles"]['waterfalls'] = ('waterfalls-near-da-lat', 'Waterfalls Near Da Lat by Motorbike: Datanla, Prenn, Elephant Falls, Pongour | Van Phu', 'Waterfalls Near Da Lat by Motorbike: Datanla, Prenn, Elephant Falls and Pongour', 'The best waterfalls near Da Lat by motorbike: Datanla, Prenn, Elephant Falls and Pongour — distances, a one-day loop, the best season and safety tips.', "Da Lat is surrounded by waterfalls, from Datanla just outside the city to wide Pongour about an hour's ride away. Here's how to see them by motorbike, which to choose and how to stay safe on wet rocks.", 'fleet-gas', '\n<h2>Why see the waterfalls by motorbike</h2>\n<p>Most of Da Lat\'s well-known waterfalls are south and southwest of the city, along roads with pine forests, coffee farms and vegetable greenhouses. With your own bike you can stop wherever you like, arrive before the tour buses and combine two or three falls in one day at your own pace.</p>\n<h2>Datanla Waterfall — the closest one</h2>\n<p>Datanla is about 5–7 km south of the center, on the road towards Prenn Pass and Tuyen Lam Lake. It\'s an easy 15-minute ride, so it fits even into a half day. Besides the cascades in the forest, Datanla is famous for its alpine coaster — a small track car that runs down through the trees to the falls, popular with families. There is an entrance ticket; buy it at the gate.</p>\n<h2>Prenn Waterfall — at the foot of the pass</h2>\n<p>Further down the same main road, at the bottom of Prenn Pass (about 10–12 km from the center), is Prenn Waterfall. It\'s a park-style site with gardens and footpaths, and a walkway lets you go behind the curtain of water. Datanla and Prenn are on the same road, so it\'s natural to visit both in one morning.</p>\n<h2>Elephant Falls — wild and powerful</h2>\n<p>Elephant Falls (Thác Voi) is near Nam Ban village, about 25–30 km southwest of Da Lat. The usual route goes over the Ta Nung pass road through hills, coffee farms and flower gardens — a beautiful but winding ride. The falls are wide and loud, and much less "arranged" than Datanla: the path down is steep, with rough steps and wet rocks. Right next to the falls is Linh An Pagoda with its large Buddha statue, worth a short stop.</p>\n<h2>Pongour Waterfall — the widest</h2>\n<p>Pongour is about 45–50 km southwest of the center, past Lien Khuong Airport. It\'s known for water flowing over many wide rock terraces — one of the most impressive waterfalls in the Central Highlands. The ride is mostly on the main highway, so it takes around 1–1.5 hours each way. Pongour is a good choice if you have a full day and want something really big.</p>\n<h2>A one-day waterfall loop</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Morning:</b> leave around 7:30, ride to Elephant Falls via Ta Nung, visit the falls and Linh An Pagoda.</li>\n<li><b>Midday:</b> continue to Pongour (about 30 km), have lunch at a local eatery on the way.</li>\n<li><b>Afternoon:</b> return to Da Lat on the highway and, if you still have energy, stop at Prenn or Datanla on the way into the city.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The whole loop is roughly 100–120 km. With less time, simply do Datanla + Prenn in a morning. More ideas are on our page <a href="{ROUTES}">What to See in Da Lat by Motorbike: 5 Routes</a>.</p>\n<h2>Best season</h2>\n<p>The falls carry the most water in the rainy season, roughly May to October–November: loud and spectacular, but the water is often brown and the paths are slippery. In the dry season (December–April) the water is clearer and the roads are dry, though some falls get thinner. Right now, in October, they are at their fullest — go in the morning, because showers usually come in the afternoon.</p>\n<h2>Safety tips</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Wear shoes with a good grip, not flip-flops; rocks near the water are always slippery.</li>\n<li>Don\'t swim and don\'t climb over barriers — currents under waterfalls are strong, especially after rain.</li>\n<li>Keep a raincoat under the seat and leave the falls before dark.</li>\n<li>On the Ta Nung pass road and Prenn Pass, slow down before bends and keep to the right.</li>\n<li>For longer trips the Honda Air Blade 125cc is the most comfortable; check fuel before leaving the city.</li>\n<li>No alcohol when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Renting a bike for your waterfall trip</h2>\n<p>All our bikes — Honda Vision 110cc, Honda Air Blade 125cc and electric scooters — cost 150,000 VND per day. No cash deposit (you leave your passport or ID card), a helmet and a phone holder included on every bike; gas scooters come with 1 liter of fuel. For rentals of 2 days or more we deliver the bike free of charge to hotels in central Da Lat. Message us on WhatsApp and we\'ll help you plan the route.</p>\n')
C["dates"]['waterfalls'] = "2026-10-12"
C["articles"]['evrules'] = ('electric-scooter-rental-rules', 'Electric Scooter Rental Rules in Da Lat | Van Phu', 'Electric Scooter Rental Rules – Van Phu Da Lat', 'Rules for renting an electric scooter in Da Lat from Van Phu: price, rental day, weight limit, battery swap, safety, damage and contacts.', 'Our electric scooters are light, quiet and made for riding around Da Lat. These are the simple rules for renting one from Van Phu — please read them before you pick up your scooter.', 'electric-row', '\n<h2>Price and pick-up</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>150,000 VND per day, paid in full at pick-up. No cash deposit.</li>\n<li>Leave your original passport or ID card. You get it back when you return the scooter in the same condition.</li>\n<li>Before you leave, take a test ride and we film the scooter\'s condition together (body, mirrors, lights, battery). We compare with this video at return.</li>\n<li>Helmets and a phone holder are included.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Rental time</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>One rental day runs from pick-up until 21:00 the same day.</li>\n<li>Returning at 21:00 the next day counts as 2 days, and so on.</li>\n<li>Returning after 21:00 is charged as one extra day. Want to extend? Message us first.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Riding rules</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Maximum 2 people, with a combined weight under 140 kg. No heavy loads.</li>\n<li>Driver and passenger wear helmets at all times.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding.</li>\n<li>Strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law: ride on the right, stop at red lights, no phone while riding.</li>\n<li>Do not lend the scooter to anyone else.</li>\n<li>Don\'t ride through deep water. In the rain, slow down — Da Lat\'s hills get slippery.</li>\n<li>Park in a guarded parking lot and lock the scooter carefully.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Battery</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>One battery lasts about 35–40 km (each bike comes with 2 batteries, about 60–70 km in total), and you start with a full one.</li>\n<li>Need more? Bring the scooter to our shop and we swap in a full battery for 20,000 VND.</li>\n<li>When about a third is left, head back or call us. Never run the battery flat — hills and two riders use more power.</li>\n<li>Avoid long, steep climbs such as Prenn Pass, Mimosa Pass and the road up Lang Biang. For longer trips a Honda Air Blade 125cc is the better choice.</li>\n<li>Don\'t use other chargers and don\'t remove the battery yourself.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Damage, loss and flat tyres</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>New damage compared with the pick-up video: you pay the actual repair cost.</li>\n<li>Lost helmet, phone holder or key: you pay the replacement price.</li>\n<li>Lost or stolen scooter: tell us and the nearest police station immediately. You pay the scooter\'s value as agreed at rental.</li>\n<li>Flat tyre: stop, push the scooter to the nearest tyre repair shop and pay for the repair. Riding on a flat damages the rim, and you pay for that damage.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Contact and return</h2>\n<p>Shop: 1/15/5 Thong Thien Hoc, Da Lat — open every day from 4:30 am. Please return the scooter before 21:00. WhatsApp/Zalo +84 944 081 101 · WhatsApp +84 834 622 149 · Telegram @vanphudalat · WeChat vanphudalat.</p>\n<p>More about our electric scooters: <a href="{EV}">electric scooter rental in Da Lat</a>. Ideas where to ride: <a href="{ROUTES}">motorbike routes around Da Lat</a>.</p>\n')
C["dates"]['evrules'] = "2026-10-14"

C["articles"]['caudat'] = ('cau-dat-tea-hills-sunrise', 'Cau Dat Tea Hills at Sunrise: Complete Guide by Motorbike | Van Phu', 'Cau Dat Tea Hills at Sunrise: A Complete Guide by Motorbike', 'How to see sunrise over the Cau Dat tea hills near Da Lat: distance, route, what time to leave, best season for clouds, what to wear and safety tips for the dark ride.', "The Cau Dat tea hills, about 20–25 km from central Da Lat, are one of the most beautiful places in the highlands to watch the sun come up over rows of tea and drifting clouds. Here's how to plan the early-morning ride.", 'airblade', '\n<h2>What Cau Dat is</h2>\n<p>Cau Dat (Cầu Đất) is a highland farming area in Xuan Truong and Tram Hanh, east of Da Lat. Tea has been grown here since the French colonial period in the 1920s, and today the slopes are covered with neat, curving rows of tea bushes, with pine forest and vegetable farms around them. Wind turbines stand on some of the nearby ridges. Because the area sits high on the plateau, early mornings are often misty, and on good days the valleys below fill with a sea of clouds.</p>\n<h2>How far it is and how to get there</h2>\n<p>The tea hills are about 20–25 km from the city center — roughly 40–60 minutes by scooter, a little more in the dark. The usual way goes east past Trai Mat and then through Xuan Tho and Xuan Truong along a winding country road with gentle climbs. Use Google Maps and search for "Cầu Đất" or "đồi chè Cầu Đất"; save the route offline before you leave, because the signal can be weak in the hills. More ideas for this direction are on our page <a href="{ROUTES}">What to See in Da Lat by Motorbike: 5 Routes</a>.</p>\n<h2>What time to leave</h2>\n<p>Sunrise in Da Lat is usually somewhere between about 5:15 and 6:00, depending on the time of year. To arrive in the soft pre-dawn light and find a good spot, leave the center around 4:30–4:45. The first light behind the hills is often the most beautiful moment, so don\'t plan to arrive exactly at sunrise. After the sun is up, the tea rows glow for another hour or so — a perfect time for photos before the light gets harsh.</p>\n<h2>Best season for clouds</h2>\n<p>Clouds are never guaranteed — they depend on humidity, wind and the night before. In general your chances are best on cool, calm mornings after rain, which is why the end of the rainy season and the early dry season (roughly October to January) are popular with cloud hunters. In the middle of the dry season the sky is often clear: fewer clouds, but crisp views and dry roads. Ask locals the evening before — we\'re happy to tell you what the mornings have been like that week.</p>\n<h2>What to bring</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A warm jacket, long trousers and gloves — before dawn it\'s much colder than in the city, and the wind on the bike makes it feel colder still.</li>\n<li>A raincoat under the seat and closed shoes; paths between the tea rows can be muddy.</li>\n<li>A fully charged phone, a small power bank and water.</li>\n<li>Some cash: a few farms and viewpoints charge a small entrance fee.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Respect the farms</h2>\n<p>The tea hills are working farms, not a park. Walk only on existing paths, don\'t step on or pick the tea bushes, don\'t fly drones over people\'s land without asking, and take all your rubbish back with you. If a farm has a gate or a ticket booth, use it.</p>\n<h2>Safety on the dark ride</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Ride slowly in the dark and in fog, with your headlight on; keep well to the right and watch for trucks carrying vegetables early in the morning.</li>\n<li>Expect a wet, slippery road after rain, and slow down before every bend.</li>\n<li>Never stop on the road for photos — pull fully off where others can see you.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Make it a morning loop</h2>\n<p>On the way back, stop at Linh Phuoc Pagoda in Trai Mat (about 8 km from the center), famous for its mosaic decoration, and be back in town for a late breakfast. In late autumn you may also see yellow wild sunflowers along the road.</p>\n<h2>Which bike to take</h2>\n<p>The round trip is about 50 km with hills, so a gas scooter is the right choice — the Honda Air Blade 125cc is the most comfortable for this ride. An electric scooter has 2 batteries (about 60–70 km), but its low power makes the long climbs slow and drains the batteries fast, so it isn\'t a good fit. Our shop opens at 4:30 am, but it\'s easiest to pick up your bike the day before. All bikes cost 150,000 VND per day, with no cash deposit (you leave your passport or ID card), a helmet and a phone holder included; gas scooters come with 1 liter of fuel. Message us on WhatsApp and we\'ll help you plan your sunrise ride.</p>\n')
C["dates"]['caudat'] = "2026-10-14"
C["articles"]['domainedemarie'] = ('domaine-de-marie-church-da-lat', 'Domaine de Marie Church Da Lat: Opening Hours, Entrance Fee & How to Get There | Van Phu', 'Domaine de Marie (Pink Church) Da Lat: Opening Hours, Entrance Fee, History & Photo Spots', 'Domaine de Marie pink church in Da Lat: opening hours, free entry, how to get there by scooter, a short history, architecture and the best photo spots — with real photos.', "The pink church on the hill is one of Da Lat's most photographed buildings — and it's free. Opening hours, how to ride there, a short history and the best photo spots, with our own photos from October 2026.", 'domaine-de-marie', '\n<h2>Quick facts</h2><ul><li>Address: 1 Ngo Quyen, Da Lat — on a small hill west of the market</li><li>Open 7:30–11:30 and 13:30–17:30 every day (Google Maps, October 2026); closed to sightseeing during Mass</li><li>Entrance: free</li><li>About 1 km from Da Lat Market and 1.6 km from Xuan Huong Lake (straight line) — under 1 km from our shop</li><li>Dress: cover shoulders and knees to enter the church</li><li>By scooter: easy on any bike, including an electric scooter</li></ul><figure style="margin:18px 0"><img src="{IMG}spots/pink-church.webp" alt="Domaine de Marie pink church Da Lat" width="750" height="1000" loading="lazy" style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:12px"><figcaption style="font-size:14px;color:#666;margin-top:6px">The front staircase of Domaine de Marie in the morning sun.</figcaption></figure><h2>Opening hours and Mass times</h2><p>You can walk around the grounds during the hours above. The church is a working place of worship: during Mass it is closed to sightseeing, and Sunday mornings are the busiest. Mass times change from time to time, so check the notice board at the gate.</p><h2>Entrance fee and parking</h2><p>Entry is free. There is motorbike parking at the site — follow the staff\'s directions and lock your bike.</p><h2>How to get there by motorbike</h2><p>From Da Lat Market it\'s only about 1 km — roughly 5 minutes by scooter. Search "Domaine de Marie" or "1 Ngo Quyen" in Google Maps. The church sits on a small hill, so the last stretch climbs gently: fine on an electric scooter (two riders under 140 kg in total) and easy on a Honda Vision 110cc or Air Blade 125cc.</p><h2>A short history</h2><p>Construction began in 1940 and the church was inaugurated in 1942. It belongs to the Daughters of Charity of Saint Vincent de Paul, a Catholic order whose sisters still live in the convent. The 3-metre statue of the Virgin Mary was designed by the French architect Jonchère in 1943 and donated by Suzanne Decoux, wife of the Governor-General of French Indochina; she died in 1944 and her tomb lies behind the church.</p><h2>Architecture</h2><p>Domaine de Marie blends French church architecture with Vietnamese touches. The pink walls have a rough, textured finish; the front is a tall triangle crowned by a cross, with a round rose window and rows of small pointed arched windows. The roofs echo the nhà rông, the communal stilt house of the Central Highlands. Inside, look for the wooden beamed ceiling, the stained glass and the statue of Mary standing on a globe, with a Vietnamese-looking face.</p><figure style="margin:18px 0"><img src="{IMG}spots/pink-convent.webp" alt="Domaine de Marie pink church Da Lat" width="1000" height="750" loading="lazy" style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:12px"><figcaption style="font-size:14px;color:#666;margin-top:6px">The convent courtyard with its flower-lined path.</figcaption></figure><h2>Best photo spots</h2><ul><li>The front staircase from a low angle — the triangle façade against a blue sky; best in the morning, when the sun lights the front.</li><li>The rose window and the arched balcony — step back and zoom in.</li><li>The convent courtyard — the straight path lined with flower pots.</li><li>The arcade — pointed arches and red stone columns along the convent.</li><li>Hydrangeas and white urns in front of the convent porch.</li></ul><figure style="margin:18px 0"><img src="{IMG}spots/pink-hydrangea.webp" alt="Domaine de Marie pink church Da Lat" width="750" height="1000" loading="lazy" style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:12px"><figcaption style="font-size:14px;color:#666;margin-top:6px">Hydrangeas in front of the convent porch.</figcaption></figure><h2>Please be respectful</h2><p>This is a place of prayer and home to the sisters. Keep your voice down, don\'t pick flowers or walk on the grass (the signs ask for this), and ask first before a wedding shoot or flying a drone.</p><h2>Nearby: a relaxed half day</h2><p>Domaine de Marie in the morning → Van Thanh flower village (about 2 km) → the Rooster Church, Da Lat Cathedral (about 2 km) → lunch around Da Lat Market. See all 16 places on our <a href="{PM}">Da Lat photo spot map</a>.</p><h2>FAQ</h2><p><b>Is Domaine de Marie free to enter?</b><br>Yes, entry is free.</p><p><b>What time does Domaine de Marie open?</b><br>Usually 7:30–11:30 and 13:30–17:30 every day (Google Maps, October 2026). It is closed to sightseeing during Mass.</p><p><b>When was Domaine de Marie built?</b><br>Construction began in 1940; it was inaugurated in 1942.</p><p><b>Is there a dress code?</b><br>Yes — cover shoulders and knees to go inside the church.</p><p><b>Can I ride there on an electric scooter?</b><br>Yes. It\'s about 1 km from the market and the last part is a gentle climb.</p><h2>Renting a scooter to get there</h2><p>Our shop is less than 1 km from Domaine de Marie. Every bike — Honda Vision 110cc, Honda Air Blade 125cc and electric scooters — costs 150,000 VND per day, with no cash deposit (you leave your passport or ID card), a helmet and a phone holder included. Prices and booking: <a href="{HOME}">motorbike rental in Da Lat</a>.</p>\n')
C["dates"]['domainedemarie'] = "2026-10-14"

C["articles"]['cloudhunt'] = ('da-lat-cloud-hunting', 'Cloud Hunting in Da Lat (Săn Mây): Where, When & How to Ride There | Van Phu', 'Cloud Hunting in Da Lat: Where to Go, What Time and How to Ride There Before Dawn', 'A practical guide to cloud hunting (săn mây) in Da Lat: the best hills and viewpoints, the best season and weather, what time to leave, what to wear and how to ride safely in the dark.', "Watching a sea of clouds roll through the valleys at sunrise is the experience many visitors come to Da Lat for. Here's how to plan it so you actually see the clouds — and ride there and back safely.", 'airblade', '\n<h2>What “cloud hunting” means</h2>\n<p>Săn mây — “cloud hunting” — is what Vietnamese travellers call getting up before dawn to watch low clouds and mist fill the valleys around Da Lat. The city sits at about 1,500 metres, and on cool, humid mornings the clouds settle below the hilltops. From the right spot you look down on a white sea with pine ridges sticking out of it, while the sun rises behind. It usually lasts only an hour or two before the warmth burns it away.</p>\n<h2>When you have the best chance</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Season:</b> clouds can appear in many months. The rainy season (roughly May–November) brings humid mornings and often the thickest clouds; the cool mornings at the end of the year are also good. In the middle of the dry season skies are often clear — beautiful sunrises, but fewer clouds.</li>\n<li><b>Weather:</b> the best mornings usually follow a rainy evening or night, with still air. Dry, windy mornings often have no clouds at all.</li>\n<li><b>Time:</b> be in place around 4:30–6:30. The show often starts before the sun comes up, so don\'t aim to arrive exactly at sunrise.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Clouds are never guaranteed. Check the forecast the evening before and keep a backup plan — even without clouds, a Da Lat sunrise is worth getting up for.</p>\n<h2>Where to go</h2>\n<p>Our free <a href="../../cloud-hunting-map/">cloud-hunting and sunrise map</a> shows 8 spots with directions. The main options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Xuan Huong Lake and Tuyen Lam Lake</b> — no early climb: mist over the water at dawn. Xuan Huong is in the city centre; Tuyen Lam is about 5 km south as the crow flies, among pine hills. Easy places to start.</li>\n<li><b>Thien Phuc Duc Hill</b> — north of the centre, known for its lone pine and a wide view over the city. The last part is a short, steep walk.</li>\n<li><b>Da Phu Hill</b> — an open hilltop above the forest north-west of town; on good mornings clouds fill the valley below. The final stretch is steep: park at the bottom and walk up.</li>\n<li><b>Hon Bo Hill</b> — the highest hill east of town, with sunrise over Thai Phien flower village. The last bit is steep red dirt — walk it rather than ride it.</li>\n<li><b>Cau Dat tea hills</b> — about 20–25 km by road, east of Da Lat: tea rows with clouds over the valley. A paid cloud-hunting viewpoint there opens at 4:30. See our <a href="../cau-dat-tea-hills-sunrise/">Cau Dat sunrise guide</a>.</li>\n<li><b>Lang Biang (Radar Peak)</b> — the highest viewpoint near Da Lat, but it opens later in the morning, so go there for morning views rather than sunrise.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What time to leave</h2>\n<p>Sunrise in Da Lat is usually between about 5:15 and 6:00, depending on the season. For the hills close to town, leave around 4:45–5:00; for Cau Dat, leave the centre around 4:15–4:30. Add time for walking the last steep part and for finding a spot before others arrive, especially on weekends.</p>\n<h2>What to wear and bring</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A warm jacket, long trousers, gloves and closed shoes — before dawn it is cold, and the wind on the bike makes it colder.</li>\n<li>A raincoat: paths and roads are often wet after a rainy night.</li>\n<li>A charged phone with the route saved offline, a small torch or phone light for the walk, and water.</li>\n<li>Some cash, as a few viewpoints charge an entrance fee.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Riding safely in the dark</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Ride slowly with your headlight on; fog can make visibility very short.</li>\n<li>Keep to the right and watch for early trucks and farm vehicles on the hill roads.</li>\n<li>Roads can be slippery after rain — brake gently and slow down before every bend.</li>\n<li>Never stop on the road to take photos; pull fully off the road.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Renting a bike for a sunrise ride</h2>\n<p>Our shop opens at 4:30 am, but it\'s easiest to pick up the bike the day before so you can leave straight from your hotel. For the hills near town, any of our bikes works, including an electric scooter (one battery lasts about 35–40 km; each bike comes with 2 batteries, about 60–70 km in total). For Cau Dat, Hon Bo or Lang Biang, take a gas scooter — the Honda Air Blade 125cc is the most comfortable on hills. Every bike is 150,000 VND per day with no cash deposit, a helmet and a phone holder included. See <a href="{HOME}">our rental page</a>, and message us on WhatsApp the evening before — we\'re happy to tell you what the mornings have been like.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-nodeposit'] = ('dalat-motorbike-rental-no-deposit', 'Motorbike Rental in Da Lat With No Cash Deposit: How It Works | Van Phu', 'Motorbike Rental in Da Lat With No Deposit in Cash: How It Works and What to Bring', 'Renting a motorbike in Da Lat with no cash deposit: you leave your passport or ID card instead. How it works, what to bring, when you get it back and simple tips.', "Many travellers search for motorbike rental in Da Lat with no deposit because they don't want to hand over a large amount of cash. Here's exactly how renting without a cash deposit works at Van Phu.", 'vision-white', '\n<h2>No cash deposit — what that means</h2>\n<p>At Van Phu we don\'t ask for a cash deposit for any bike: Honda Vision 110cc, Honda Air Blade 125cc or electric scooter. Instead, you leave your passport (or, for Vietnamese guests, your ID card) at the shop while you have the bike. When you bring the bike back, you get your document back straight away. So motorbike rental in Da Lat with no deposit in cash is simply how we always work — not a special offer.</p>\n<p>The rental itself is paid upfront: 150,000 VND per day for every bike, a fixed price. Our <a href="../dalat-motorbike-rental-price/">rental price guide</a> explains exactly what is included.</p>\n<h2>Why a document instead of cash</h2>\n<p>A cash deposit means carrying a lot of money, counting it twice and worrying about getting it back. A document is simpler for both sides: you don\'t need to withdraw extra cash, and we know the bike will come back. It\'s a common way of renting scooters in Vietnam.</p>\n<h2>What to bring</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Your passport</b> (foreign guests) or <b>ID card</b> (Vietnamese guests).</li>\n<li>The rental fee for the days you need.</li>\n<li>Your phone with WhatsApp, Zalo, Telegram or WeChat, so we can stay in touch during the rental.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Tip: take a photo of your passport and keep it on your phone. Many hotels in Vietnam ask to see or scan your passport at check-in, so it\'s easiest to check in first and pick up the bike afterwards.</p>\n<h2>How it works step by step</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Message us with your dates and the bike you\'d like.</li>\n<li>At pick-up, you take a short test ride and we film a short video of the bike\'s condition together.</li>\n<li>You leave your passport or ID card and pay the rental fee.</li>\n<li>You get a helmet and a phone holder; gas scooters come with 1 liter of fuel.</li>\n<li>Bring the bike back by 21:00 on your last day — a rental day runs from pick-up until 21:00 — and your document is returned to you on the spot.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Is my passport safe?</h2>\n<p>Your document is kept safely at the shop and only returned to you, the person who rented the bike. Before you leave, check that the name on the booking matches your passport, and keep the shop\'s contacts saved in your phone. If your plans change and you need your passport earlier — for example for a flight or a bus ticket — just tell us and return the bike first.</p>\n<h2>Good to know</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The video at pick-up protects you: it shows the bike\'s condition before you ride.</li>\n<li>If you\'re running late on your return day, message us before 21:00.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Book without a cash deposit</h2>\n<p>Send us your dates on WhatsApp (+84 944 081 101) or Zalo (0944 081 101) and we\'ll keep a bike for you. All bikes, prices and conditions are on our <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>; if you\'re choosing an electric scooter, see the <a href="{EV}">electric scooter page</a> too.</p>\n')
