C["articles"]['selfguided'] = ('dalat-self-guided-motorbike-route', 'Dalat Self Guided Motorbike Route: Explore Without a Tour | Van Phu', 'Dalat Self-Guided Motorbike Route: How to Explore Da Lat Without a Tour', 'Planning a Dalat self guided motorbike route? Offline maps, fuel and battery, timing around the weather, safety and how to build your own day — no tour needed.', "You don't need a tour to see Da Lat. With a rented scooter, a phone and a little planning you can build your own day around the lakes, pine hills and cafés — at your own pace. Here is how to plan a self-guided motorbike day in Da Lat so it runs smoothly.", 'white-honda-vision-sidewalk-dalat', '\n<h2>Why explore Da Lat on your own</h2>\n<p>Most of Da Lat\'s best places — waterfalls, Tuyen Lam Lake, flower farms, the tea hills, viewpoints over the pine forest — are spread around the city, roughly 5–25 km from the centre. A tour takes you to a fixed list on a fixed schedule. On your own scooter you choose where to stop, how long to stay and when to turn back if the clouds roll in. That freedom is the main reason so many travellers plan a self-guided motorbike route in Dalat.</p>\n<h2>Step 1: pick one direction per day</h2>\n<p>The easiest way to plan is to choose one direction and stay in it, instead of crossing the whole city several times:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>South:</b> Datanla Waterfall, Tuyen Lam Lake and Truc Lam Monastery — close together and easy for a first day.</li>\n<li><b>North:</b> the flower villages and Lang Biang area — wide valleys and quiet roads.</li>\n<li><b>East:</b> Trai Mat and Linh Phuoc Pagoda, and further on the Cau Dat tea hills for a longer day.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Our page <a href="{ROUTES}">What to See in Da Lat by Motorbike</a> describes these directions with distances, so you can copy one as your base and adjust it.</p>\n<h2>Step 2: get your phone ready</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Download an offline map</b> of the Da Lat area in Google Maps (or another offline map app) while you have Wi-Fi. Mobile signal is usually fine, but it can drop in valleys and forest.</li>\n<li><b>Save your stops and your hotel</b> as pins, plus our shop at 1/15/5 Thong Thien Hoc.</li>\n<li><b>Bring a power bank</b> — navigation drains the battery fast, and cold mornings make it worse.</li>\n<li>Every bike has a phone holder, so you can follow the map without stopping.</li>\n</ul>\n{PHOTO:honda-vision-bui-thi-xuan-street-dalat}\n<h2>Step 3: fuel or battery</h2>\n<p>Gas scooters come with 1 liter of fuel — enough to reach a petrol station. Fill up in the city before heading out; stations are easy to find on main roads, but rarer on small country lanes. With an electric scooter, keep your loop compact: one battery lasts about 35–40 km; each scooter comes with 2 batteries, about 60–70 km in total, and you can swap at the shop for 20,000 VND. For long climbs like Prenn, Mimosa or Lang Biang, choose a Honda Air Blade 125cc.</p>\n<h2>Step 4: plan around the weather and daylight</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Start early. Mornings are usually the clearest, and in the rainy season showers often come in the afternoon.</li>\n<li>It gets dark early in Da Lat, and hill roads are not lit. Plan to be back in the city before sunset.</li>\n<li>Pack a jacket and a raincoat even if the morning is sunny — it can be cold and the weather changes quickly in the hills.</li>\n<li>Leave room in the plan: three or four stops make a relaxed day; seven make a rushed one.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Step 5: ride safely</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right. Ride steadily, use your horn lightly before blind bends, and let faster traffic pass.</li>\n<li>On descents, slow down early and use both brakes gently.</li>\n<li>Never stop on a bend for a photo — pull fully off the road.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n<li>If something feels wrong with the bike, stop somewhere safe and message us.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>New to riding in Vietnam? Read our <a href="{ART:riding}">motorbike riding tips</a> first. If you prefer a ready-made plan, the <a href="{ART:days2}">2-day itinerary</a> works well as a self-guided trip too.</p>\n<h2>Rent a scooter for your own route</h2>\n<p>Honda Vision 110cc, Honda Air Blade 125cc or electric scooter — all 150,000 VND per day, with a helmet and phone holder, and no cash deposit (you leave your passport or ID card). Tell us where you plan to go and we\'ll suggest the right bike and a few local tips. Book on our <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-2days'] = ('rent-scooter-dalat-2-days', 'Rent a Scooter in Dalat for 2 Days: How It Works | Van Phu', 'Rent a Scooter in Dalat for 2 Days: How the Days Are Counted and a Simple Plan', 'Want to rent a scooter in Dalat for 2 days? How the 2 days are counted, free hotel delivery in central Da Lat, what is included and a simple 2-day plan.', 'Two days is the most popular rental length in Da Lat: enough time for the waterfalls, a lake, a sunrise and the night market, without rushing. Here is exactly how it works when you rent a scooter in Dalat for 2 days with Van Phu.', 'vision-air-blade-villa-delivery-dalat', '\n<h2>How the 2 days are counted</h2>\n<p>A rental day runs from the moment you pick up the bike until 21:00 that day. So for a 2-day rental, you collect the bike on day one and return it by 21:00 on day two. Picking up early in the morning gives you the most riding time — our shop opens at 4:30 am, which also works for a sunrise ride.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pick up Saturday at 8:00 → return by Sunday 21:00 = 2 days.</li>\n<li>If you\'re running late on the return day, message us before 21:00.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Price and what is included</h2>\n<p>Every bike costs 150,000 VND per day, a fixed price, so 2 days is 300,000 VND. Every rental includes a helmet and a phone holder; gas scooters come with 1 liter of fuel. There is no cash deposit — you leave your passport (or ID card for Vietnamese guests). For full details see our <a href="../dalat-motorbike-rental-price/">rental price guide</a>.</p>\n<h2>Free hotel delivery from 2 days</h2>\n<p>When you rent a scooter in Dalat for 2 days or more, we deliver the bike free of charge to hotels in central Da Lat. Send us your hotel name and the time you\'d like the bike, and we bring it to you. At handover we check the bike together: a short test ride and a video of its condition. On the last day we can collect it from the same hotel — just agree the time with us.</p>\n{PHOTO:honda-vision-hotel-delivery-dalat}\n<h2>Which bike for a 2-day trip</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Honda Air Blade 125cc:</b> the best choice if you plan the Cau Dat tea hills, Lang Biang, the Prenn or Mimosa passes, or riding as two.</li>\n<li><b>Honda Vision 110cc:</b> light and easy, perfect for the city and nearby sights.</li>\n<li><b>Electric scooter:</b> quiet and simple for the city; one battery lasts about 35–40 km and each scooter comes with 2 batteries, about 60–70 km in total. Not for long steep passes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>A simple 2-day plan</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Day 1:</b> the south side — Datanla Waterfall, Tuyen Lam Lake and Truc Lam Monastery — then the centre and the night market in the evening.</li>\n<li><b>Day 2:</b> an early start for sunrise and the tea hills or a flower village, coffee with a view, and the bike back by 21:00.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The full route with timings is in our <a href="{ART:days2}">Da Lat 2-day itinerary by motorbike</a>.</p>\n{PHOTO:air-blade-handover-dalat-homestay}\n<h2>Tips for a 2-day rental</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Book a day or two ahead at weekends and on holidays.</li>\n<li>Keep a raincoat under the seat and a warm layer for the mornings.</li>\n<li>Park in guarded parking where there is one and always take the key.</li>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right; no alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Book your 2 days</h2>\n<p>Message us on WhatsApp, Zalo, Telegram or WeChat with your dates, the bike you like and your hotel. Everything about our bikes is on the <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-ev-couples'] = ('electric-scooter-for-two-da-lat', 'Electric Scooter for Two in Da Lat: Couples Guide | Van Phu', 'Renting an Electric Scooter for Two in Da Lat: What Couples Should Know', 'Renting an electric scooter for two in Da Lat: max 2 riders under 140 kg combined, comfort tips, battery planning, and when the Air Blade 125cc is better.', "Riding together on a quiet electric scooter is a lovely way to see Da Lat's lakes, cafés and flower-lined streets. But two people on one scooter in a hilly city needs a bit of planning. Here is what couples should know before renting an electric scooter for two in Da Lat.", 'electric-scooters-helmets-van-phu', '\n<h2>Can two people ride one electric scooter?</h2>\n<p>Yes — our electric scooters take a maximum of 2 riders, with a combined weight under 140 kg. This is a safety rule for the motor, brakes and battery, so please tell us honestly when you book. If the two of you are over 140 kg together, we\'ll suggest a Honda Air Blade 125cc instead, at the same price.</p>\n<h2>Where an electric scooter for two works well</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Around Xuan Huong Lake and the city centre.</li>\n<li>Café hopping and short rides between central sights.</li>\n<li>An evening ride to the night market — park and walk around together.</li>\n<li>Gentle loops close to the city at a relaxed pace.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The scooter is quiet and has no gears, so the passenger can talk, take photos and enjoy the ride.</p>\n<h2>Battery: plan a little more carefully as two</h2>\n<p>One battery lasts about 35–40 km; each scooter comes with 2 batteries, about 60–70 km in total. With a passenger and Da Lat\'s hills, the battery goes down faster than with one rider, so keep your loop compact and start heading back when about a third is left. A battery swap at our shop costs 20,000 VND.</p>\n<h2>When to choose the Air Blade instead</h2>\n<p>Long steep passes such as Prenn and Mimosa, the road up Lang Biang, and long day trips to the Cau Dat tea hills or the big waterfalls are not suitable for an electric scooter with two people. For those days the Honda Air Blade 125cc is the right bike for couples: more power on climbs, a comfortable seat for two and no battery to think about — still 150,000 VND per day.</p>\n{PHOTO:van-phu-guest-air-blade-thumbs-up}\n<h2>Comfort and safety tips for two</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Both riders wear a helmet — we give you two. Fasten the strap every time.</li>\n<li>The passenger sits still and leans with the rider on bends, feet on the footrests.</li>\n<li>Pull away gently: with two people the scooter feels heavier at the start and on hills.</li>\n<li>Brake earlier and more softly downhill, especially in rain; never ride through deep water.</li>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right. No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n<li>Bring a jacket for both of you — Da Lat evenings and mornings are cool.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All conditions are listed in our <a href="{ART:evrules}">electric scooter rental rules</a>.</p>\n<h2>Rent an electric scooter for two</h2>\n<p>150,000 VND per day with 2 batteries, two helmets and a phone holder, and no cash deposit — you leave your passport or ID card. At pick-up we do a short test ride and film the scooter\'s condition together. See the scooters on our <a href="{EV}">electric scooter page</a> or compare all bikes on the <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>.</p>\n')
