C["articles"]['rainyday'] = ('what-to-do-in-da-lat-on-a-rainy-day', 'What to Do in Da Lat on a Rainy Day: Ideas & Riding Tips | Van Phu', 'What to Do in Da Lat on a Rainy Day: Indoor Ideas and Riding Tips', 'Rain in Da Lat? Cafés, the covered market, museums and palaces, hot pot, and how to ride safely between showers — a practical rainy-day plan.', "From roughly May to November, Da Lat gets regular rain — usually in the afternoon, often heavy but short. A wet day doesn't have to be a lost day. Here is how to plan it, what to do while it pours, and how to ride safely between showers.", 'group-rental-air-blade-van-phu-morning', '<h2>How rain usually works in Da Lat</h2>\n<p>In the rainy season, mornings are often dry and bright, clouds build up around midday and the rain comes in the afternoon. Some days it rains for an hour, some days it drizzles on and off until evening. So the simple rule is: <b>do the outdoor sights and longer rides in the morning, keep indoor plans for the afternoon</b>. Our guide to <a href="{ART:weather}">Da Lat weather by month</a> explains the seasons in more detail.</p>\n<h2>Indoor and covered ideas in the city</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Café hopping:</b> Da Lat has hundreds of cafés, many with big windows over pine hills or Xuan Huong Lake. Watching the rain with a hot coffee or an artichoke tea is a very Da Lat thing to do. Ideas by area are in our <a href="{ART:coffee}">cafés with a view</a> guide.</li>\n<li><b>Da Lat Market (Chợ Đà Lạt):</b> the main market building is covered — dried fruit, candied jams, tea, artichoke products and local snacks.</li>\n<li><b>Lam Dong Museum:</b> a good introduction to the history of the plateau and the ethnic groups of the Central Highlands.</li>\n<li><b>Bao Dai\'s Summer Palace (Dinh III):</b> the last emperor\'s 1930s villa, with original rooms and furniture — mostly indoors.</li>\n<li><b>Da Lat Railway Station:</b> the old French-era station with its colourful hall and old carriages; there is cover while you wait for a shower to pass.</li>\n<li><b>Churches and pagodas:</b> the <a href="{ART:domainedemarie}">Domaine de Marie church</a> and Linh Phuoc Pagoda have indoor halls worth a slow visit.</li>\n</ul>\n{PHOTO:honda-vision-cafe-garden-dalat}\n<h2>Warm food for a cool, wet day</h2>\n<p>Rainy weather is hot-pot weather. Look for lẩu gà lá é (chicken hot pot with a local herb), grilled skewers, bánh căn (small rice-flour pancakes) and a cup of hot soy milk with bánh mì nướng in the evening. Our <a href="{ART:food}">Da Lat food guide</a> lists what to try.</p>\n<h2>What to skip when it pours</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Waterfalls and steep trails:</b> paths and steps get slippery, and water levels can rise quickly.</li>\n<li><b>Long mountain passes:</b> on Prenn, Mimosa and the road to Lang Biang, heavy rain means poor visibility and slippery bends. Go another day.</li>\n<li><b>Sunrise and cloud-hunting rides:</b> check the sky the night before; in steady rain you will not see much anyway.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Riding a scooter in the rain: practical tips</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Keep a raincoat (áo mưa) under the seat — they are sold cheaply all over Da Lat. A poncho-style one covers a passenger too.</li>\n<li>Ride slowly, brake gently with both brakes, and take bends wide and calm. Painted lines and manhole covers are very slippery.</li>\n<li>Never ride through deep or flowing water — turn around or wait.</li>\n<li>Turn your lights on in rain and fog, keep a bigger distance and stay to the right.</li>\n<li>In a heavy downpour, simply stop at a café and wait — it usually passes.</li>\n<li>Put your phone and passport copy in a plastic bag; the phone holder still works for Google Maps.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>More general advice is in our <a href="{ART:riding}">tips for riding a motorbike in Vietnam</a>.</p>\n<h2>A simple rainy-day plan</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Morning:</b> a dry-weather sight close to town while the sky is clear — the railway station, a pagoda or a lakeside loop.</li>\n<li><b>Lunch:</b> hot pot or noodle soup near the centre.</li>\n<li><b>Afternoon:</b> museum or Bao Dai\'s palace, then a long café stop while the rain falls.</li>\n<li><b>Evening:</b> if it clears, a short ride to the night market; if not, grilled food near your hotel.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Rent a scooter for short hops between showers</h2>\n<p>On rainy days a scooter is the easiest way to move between cafés, the market and museums without waiting for a car. At Văn Phú every bike costs 150,000 VND per day, with a free helmet and a phone holder, and no cash deposit — you leave your passport or ID card. The Honda Vision is light and easy on wet city streets. See all bikes on our <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-foreigners'] = ('can-foreigners-rent-a-motorbike-in-dalat', "Can Foreigners Rent a Motorbike in Dalat? Yes — Here's How | Van Phu", 'Can Foreigners Rent a Motorbike in Dalat? What You Need and How It Works', 'Can foreigners rent a motorbike in Dalat? Yes: passport only, no cash deposit, booking in English, Honda Vision, Air Blade or electric scooter at 150,000 VND/day.', "It's one of the first questions travellers ask us: can foreigners rent a motorbike in Dalat? The short answer is yes. Here is exactly what you need, how booking in English works and which bike suits your plans.", 'friends-air-blade-van-phu-gate', '<h2>Can foreigners rent a motorbike in Dalat? Yes</h2>\n<p>Most of our guests are travellers from abroad. You can rent any of our bikes — the Honda Vision 110cc, the Honda Air Blade 125cc or an electric scooter — on the same terms as Vietnamese guests. Every bike costs a fixed 150,000 VND per day.</p>\n<h2>What you need: your passport</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Passport:</b> foreign guests leave their passport with us while they have the bike. You get it back when you return the bike.</li>\n<li><b>No cash deposit:</b> you don\'t leave any money as a deposit — only the passport.</li>\n<li><b>Payment:</b> you pay the rental for your days at pick-up.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>That\'s all. The full step-by-step process is in <a href="{ART:lt-procedure}">how to rent a motorbike in Da Lat</a>, and more about the passport rule is in <a href="{ART:lt-nodeposit}">motorbike rental with no cash deposit</a>.</p>\n<h2>Booking in English (or your language)</h2>\n<p>You don\'t need to speak Vietnamese. Message us on WhatsApp (+84 944 081 101 or +84 834 622 149), Telegram (@vanphudalat), WeChat (vanphudalat) or Zalo (0944 081 101). Send:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>your dates and roughly what time you want to pick up;</li>\n<li>which bike you\'d like, or what you plan to do so we can suggest one;</li>\n<li>how many riders.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The shop is at 1/15/5 Thông Thiên Học in central Da Lat and opens at 4:30 am, which helps if you want to catch the sunrise. For rentals of 2 days or more we deliver the bike free to hotels in central Da Lat; for one day, just message us and ask.</p>\n{PHOTO:guest-white-honda-vision-van-phu-shop}\n<h2>Which bike suits a foreign traveller?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Honda Vision 110cc:</b> light, low and fully automatic — the easiest choice for the city and for riders who feel less confident.</li>\n<li><b>Honda Air Blade 125cc:</b> more power for hills, two riders and day trips to the tea hills, waterfalls or Lang Biang.</li>\n<li><b>Electric scooter:</b> quiet and simple for city rides; one battery lasts about 35–40 km and each scooter comes with 2 batteries, about 60–70 km in total. Read <a href="{ART:lt-ev-foreigners}">electric scooter rental for foreigners</a> if that\'s your choice.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What happens at pick-up</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>We show you the bike and how it works, then you take a short test ride.</li>\n<li>We film the bike\'s condition together, so nobody argues later.</li>\n<li>You get a free helmet; there is a phone holder on every bike, and gas scooters come with 1 liter of fuel.</li>\n<li>A rental day runs from pick-up until 21:00.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Riding tips for visitors</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right — take a few quiet minutes to get used to it.</li>\n<li>Wear your helmet and fasten the strap every time.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n<li>Da Lat is hilly: brake gently with both brakes going downhill, and slow down in rain or fog.</li>\n<li>Use guarded parking (gửi xe) and keep the ticket.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Ready to ride?</h2>\n<p>So, can foreigners rent a motorbike in Dalat? Absolutely — bring your passport and message us your dates. See prices and all bikes on our <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-ev-beginners'] = ('electric-scooter-for-beginners-da-lat', 'Electric Scooter for Beginners in Da Lat: First-Ride Guide | Van Phu', 'Electric Scooter for Beginners in Da Lat: How to Start Riding Calmly', 'Never ridden a scooter? An electric scooter for beginners in Da Lat: no gears, quiet, light. How the controls work, where to practise and first-ride tips.', "If you have never ridden a scooter, Da Lat's hills can look a little scary. An electric scooter is often the gentlest way to start: no gears, no engine noise, light to push. Here is how a beginner can get comfortable on an electric scooter in Da Lat, step by step.", 'electric-scooters-van-phu-shop', '<h2>Why an electric scooter is good for beginners</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>No gears, no clutch:</b> you only use the throttle and the two brakes.</li>\n<li><b>Quiet and smooth:</b> no engine vibration, so it\'s easier to hear traffic and stay relaxed.</li>\n<li><b>Light:</b> easy to push, park and turn around when you take a wrong street.</li>\n<li><b>No fuel stops:</b> one battery lasts about 35–40 km; each scooter comes with 2 batteries, about 60–70 km in total — plenty for a day in the city.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>The controls in one minute</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Right grip:</b> the throttle. Twist it gently — an electric motor pulls away straight away, without the short delay of a gas scooter.</li>\n<li><b>Right lever:</b> front brake. <b>Left lever:</b> rear brake. Use both together, softly.</li>\n<li><b>Switch it on</b> with the key, keep your hand off the throttle until both feet are ready, then roll off slowly.</li>\n<li><b>Lights, indicators and horn</b> are on the handlebar — we show you each one before you leave.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Your first ride, step by step</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>At our shop we explain the scooter, then you take a short test ride in front of the shop with us watching.</li>\n<li>We film the scooter\'s condition together before you go.</li>\n<li>Start on flat, quiet streets in the morning, when traffic is lighter.</li>\n<li>Practise starting, stopping and slow turns until it feels natural — ten minutes is often enough.</li>\n<li>Only then ride towards Xuan Huong Lake and the busier centre.</li>\n</ul>\n{PHOTO:guest-white-electric-scooter-dalat}\n<h2>Hills: the main thing beginners should know</h2>\n<p>Da Lat is built on hills, even in the centre. Going up, keep a steady throttle. Going down, ease off early and brake gently with both brakes, slowing down before bends rather than in them. Long steep passes such as Prenn, Mimosa and the road up Lang Biang are not for electric scooters — and not for a first ride either. For those trips later, choose a Honda Air Blade 125cc at the same price.</p>\n<h2>Traffic tips for first-time riders</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right. Keep to the right side of the lane and let faster bikes pass.</li>\n<li>A horn usually means "I\'m here", not anger — stay calm and keep a steady line.</li>\n<li>Slow down at roundabouts and intersections and look around, even on green.</li>\n<li>Avoid sudden moves; predictable riding keeps you safe.</li>\n<li>Don\'t carry a passenger until you feel confident. The limit is 2 riders under 140 kg combined.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n<li>Use the phone holder for Google Maps — never hold your phone while riding.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All conditions are listed in our <a href="{ART:evrules}">electric scooter rental rules</a>, and more practical advice is in <a href="{ART:lt-ev-experience}">tips for renting an electric scooter in Da Lat</a>.</p>\n<h2>Good first routes for beginners</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A slow loop around Xuan Huong Lake.</li>\n<li>The flower park and the streets around the lake\'s north side.</li>\n<li>Da Lat Railway Station, then back to the centre for coffee.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Keep rides short, park in guarded lots (gửi xe) and head back when about a third of the battery is left. A battery swap at our shop costs 20,000 VND.</p>\n<h2>Rent an electric scooter for your first ride</h2>\n<p>150,000 VND per day, free helmet, phone holder, and no cash deposit — you leave your passport or ID card. See the scooters on our <a href="{EV}">electric scooter rental page</a> or compare every bike on the <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>.</p>\n')
